        dek: "Virtue is the only good. The claim that made Stoicism — and the claim most likely to be rejected by anyone who lives in a world of systems, not just souls.",
        objective: "The student can reconstruct Zeno's argument for virtue as the only good and locate one place where systemic analysis challenges the individual-ethics frame.",
        parentBriefing: "This is the philosophical core. Zeno's argument: only virtue can't be misused, so only virtue is good. The emerging student will see the gap: systemic injustice means individual virtue can be structurally futile. Don't resolve it for them. Let them sit in the tension.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't let 'virtue is the only good' become 'so systemic change doesn't matter.' The Stoics didn't say that — they said virtue INCLUDES working for justice.", "If they're cynical about systems, that's the right instinct. Don't fix it with a slogan. Ask what virtue looks like inside a broken system."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The axiom and its test", body: "Zeno's argument, reconstructed from Cicero and Diogenes Laërtius: a good must always benefit its possessor. Health, wealth, and pleasure can harm — they can be misused. Only virtue — the disposition to use everything well — cannot be misused, because it IS good use. Therefore only virtue is good. The reasoning is rigorous. The problem is that it locates the good entirely in the individual soul. A student living in a world of structural inequality will ask: what good is individual virtue when the system is unjust? The Stoic answer is that working for justice IS virtue — justice is one of the four cardinal virtues. But the tension between an ethics of the soul and an ethics of systems is real, and Zeno doesn't fully resolve it. That gap is your curriculum." }],
        bigIdea: "Virtue is the only good — but the Stoics never said systemic justice doesn't matter. They said it's a virtue.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Axiom vs. system", minutes: 16, steps: ["Reconstruct Zeno's argument in four steps (a good must always benefit; externals can harm; virtue can't harm; therefore virtue is the only good).", "Name one systemic problem where individual virtue seems insufficient.", "Argue: what does Stoic justice look like inside that system?", "Practice/Reflect: where does the individual-ethics frame break down, and what do you do there?"] }],
