        parentBriefing: "Zeno called them 'logos spermaticos' — seeds of reason. Every person has them. They grow when you practice thinking well. Keep it concrete: a seed is a small good thought. A grown plant is a wise choice. The tool can water the seed with a hint, but it cannot be the seed.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't make this abstract. 'Seeds of reason' is a metaphor for small good thoughts — keep it tangible.", "Every person has seeds — including people you disagree with. Don't use this to say 'everyone is basically good so bad things don't count.'"],
        reading: [{ heading: "Tiny seeds, big trees", body: "Zeno said something amazing: inside every person, there are tiny seeds of reason. Not big wisdom — tiny seeds. A seed is a small good thought: 'That's not fair.' 'I should try again.' 'I don't really know.' These are seeds. If you practice, they grow into wise choices. If you ignore them, they stay seeds. A talking tool can give you a hint — that's like watering the seed. But the seed is yours. It was there before the tool. It will be there after." }],
        bigIdea: "Good thinking starts tiny. Practice makes it grow.",
