        parentBriefing: "This sitting is personal. The Stoic claim that everyone has seeds of reason is easy to endorse in theory. In practice — with a difficult co-parent, a hostile relative, a political opponent — it's one of the hardest things to live. The question isn't whether Zeno was right. It's whether you're willing to act as if he was.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't use this to justify staying in a toxic situation. Seeing seeds of reason doesn't mean tolerating abuse. It means knowing the difference between someone who can't reason and someone who won't.", "Your seeds need cultivation too. If you're always looking for seeds in others and ignoring your own, the garden is one-sided."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The adult garden", body: "Zeno said every person has seeds of reason. The adult version of this claim is the hardest to live: it applies to the person you've stopped listening to. The co-parent who won't compromise. The relative who won't reason. The political view that seems beyond reach. The Stoic position is not that these people are right — it's that the capacity for reason exists in them, dormant or ignored. Whether you cultivate it is your choice, not theirs. But the claim has a limit: a seed that has been actively destroyed is not the same as a seed that hasn't been watered. Knowing the difference — can this person reason but won't, or can this person no longer reason — is wisdom, not pessimism." }],
