        parentBriefing: "Keep it concrete. 'Living according to nature' for a child means: things work a certain way, and fighting it makes you tired. You need sleep — that's nature. You need food — that's nature. People feel sad sometimes — that's nature too. The practice is working with how things are, not wishing they were different.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't use 'nature' to mean 'whatever happens is fine.' Zeno meant: understand how things actually work, then choose well within that.", "Don't say 'it's natural so it's good.' Getting sick is natural. That doesn't make it good. It makes it real — and you work with it."],
        reading: [{ heading: "Working with what's real", body: "Zeno said the best life is living according to nature. What does that mean? It means: things work a certain way. Rain falls down, not up. Seeds need dirt and water to grow. People need sleep and food and kindness. Fighting how things work makes you tired. Working with how things work makes you strong. You don't have to like everything that's real. You have to understand it and then choose what to do. That's living according to nature — not letting things happen, but knowing how they happen and choosing well inside that." }],
        bigIdea: "Things work a certain way. Work with it, not against it.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "What's real?", minutes: 8, steps: ["Name three things that work a certain way in nature (rain, sleep, seeds).", "Name one way you fight how things work (staying up late, eating poorly).", "Ask a grown-up: what's one way you work with nature?", "Try this: work with one real thing today instead of against it."] }],
        dinnerQuestion: "What is one thing that works a certain way that we can work with?",
