        hardEdges: ["Don't dramatize self-death. If they ask, say plainly: 'He was old and chose to go. That was his choice. What matters is what he left behind.' Don't elaborate.", "Don't make the founder a saint. Zeno had a temper, made mistakes, and his school argued about his ideas for centuries. He was a person, not a monument."],
        reading: [{ heading: "What lasts", body: "Zeno taught on the Painted Porch in Athens for almost 50 years. He started with nothing — a shipwreck, a book, a barefoot teacher. He built a school that lasted 500 years. When he was very old, the story says he chose to leave. But what he built stayed. His ideas — virtue is the only good, the world is one city, everyone has seeds of reason — outlived him by centuries. That's the lesson: what you build with your choices can last longer than you. A talking tool can tell you about Zeno. But the building is yours." }],
        bigIdea: "What you build with your choices can last longer than you.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "What lasts?", minutes: 8, steps: ["Name one thing you've built this week (a drawing, a kind act, a good habit).", "Ask: will it last longer than today? What about a week? A year?", "Ask a grown-up: what have you built that lasted?", "Try this: build one small thing today that could last."] }],
        dinnerQuestion: "What is one thing we've built as a family that will last?",
