        objective: "The student can distinguish between Zeno's personal death and the philosophy he founded, and evaluate whether the founder's biography determines the truth of the teaching.",
        parentBriefing: "The death is tricky. Ancient sources say Zeno chose to die — possibly by holding his breath, possibly by refusing food. This was seen as a Stoic act: choosing when to leave a life that could no longer be lived according to nature. But that's a personal decision, not a philosophical proof. The question is whether the founder's death validates the teaching or is just part of his story.",
        hardEdges: ["Don't glamorize self-death. The Stoic position on suicide is nuanced: it's permissible when nature no longer allows virtuous living, not as an escape from difficulty. This is NOT a recommendation.", "If the student is struggling with mental health, stop the philosophy immediately. This sitting is not about them. Get a professional."],
        reading: [{ heading: "The death and the teaching", body: "Zeno died, by ancient account, by his own choice. He was old, possibly ill, and decided his natural life was complete. The Stoic tradition treated this as consistent with the philosophy: a rational being can choose when a life that can no longer be lived according to nature is complete. But here's the critical distinction: Zeno's death is a personal act, not a philosophical proof. The fact that the founder died in a way consistent with his teaching tells us he believed it. It doesn't tell us the teaching is true. Epictetus was a slave. Seneca was wealthy. Marcus was an emperor. All lived (and died) differently. The philosophy is tested by how it works across lives, not by how the founder exited his." }],
        bigIdea: "The founder's death shows he believed his philosophy. It doesn't prove the philosophy is true.",
        tryThis: [{ title: "Separate the life from the teaching", minutes: 12, steps: ["Write two columns: ZENO'S LIFE / ZENO'S TEACHING.", "Life: shipwreck, taught on the Stoa for 50 years, chose to die old.", "Teaching: virtue is the only good, live according to nature, the world is one city.", "Talk About It: does the death make the teaching more or less credible? Or is it irrelevant to whether it's true?"] }],
        dinnerQuestion: "Does the way someone lives (or dies) make their ideas more true — or just more believed?",
