        reading: [{ heading: "The legend and the residue", body: "Zeno's biography comes primarily from Diogenes Laërtius, writing 500 years after Zeno's death. The shipwreck is plausible — Phoenician merchants did lose ships, and Zeno was from Citium, a Phoenician colony. The teaching on the Stoa is well-attested across multiple sources. The self-death is reported but varies in detail across accounts. The founding story — shipwreck, bookshop, Crates walking by — has the structure of an origin myth: too perfect to be entirely invented, too neat to be entirely factual. The philosophical question: does it matter? Stoicism is not validated by Zeno's biography. It's validated by 500 years of practitioners who tested it against their lives. Epictetus tested it as a slave. Marcus tested it as an emperor. Seneca tested it as a politician. The philosophy survives demythologizing because it was never founded on the myth — it was founded on the argument." }],
